% ============================================================================
% Constraint C11: status/revocation-propagation mismatch  [EXERCISED 2026-07-28, check -k]
% ============================================================================
% Paper C11 / vault CPT-static_dynamic_revocation_mismatch (static-dynamic-revocation-mismatch), the taxonomy
% instrument for the status/revocation-propagation design-error class (CPT-design_error_status_revocation_propagation).
% Two claims with mismatched revocation lifespans must not share a credential:
% revoking a dynamic claim would needlessly revoke a co-located static one.
% Dual of C10 (dependent-claim bundling, CPT-dependent_claim_bundling), which forces co-location.
%
% Status (exercised 2026-07-28, re-scoped and re-exercised 2026-08-05 with
% refinery-cli check -k):
%   - revocation_mismatch_instance.problem (chained two-claim pair, one static-
%     one dynamic-marked) -> "Inconsistencies found in model:
%     revocation_mismatch::revocation_mismatch(claim_0_0, claim_0_1): error."
%   - revocation_mismatch_control.problem (same structure, both static-marked)
%     -> "Model is consistent". The inconsistency is therefore attributable to
%     this error predicate, not to the chained structure.
%   - probe_c11_separated_design.problem (the prescribed remedy: the two
%     mismatched claims in SEPARATE credentials of one subject) -> "Model is
%     consistent". Before the 2026-08-05 colocated_step re-scoping this probe
%     was inconsistent, which is the defect the re-scoping repairs.
%   - probe_c11_separated_design_control.problem (same, single cadence) ->
%     "Model is consistent".
%
% Remaining open modeling question:
%   - The base metamodel (vc_metamodel.refinery) has NO revocation/lifecycle
%     structure. This file adds the minimal new structure: two marker classes
%     (StaticRevocation, DynamicRevocation) each targeting a Claim. Whether
%     revocation cadence belongs under GovernanceAnnotation, its own annotation
%     family, or as a Claim attribute is UNRESOLVED; kept standalone here to
%     avoid mislabeling it FSL (GovernanceAnnotation is in the FSL shadow pred).
%
% Encoding note ("same credential"):
%   - "Same credential" for two claims is NOT expressible as a shared source:
%     Claim declares `contains CredEntity[1] source`, so a CredEntity has at
%     most one container and two distinct claims can never share one source
%     (Refinery containment; this is why the CSOK instance gives each claim its
%     own CredentialSubject, related only by the `aligned` shadow pred). Two
%     claims are co-located instead when both are reachable from the same
%     credential root through credential_statement edges AND chain-link
%     alignment steps (`colocated_step+` below); the chained ED shape connects
%     a child credential to its parent only through the alignment step.
%   - Since 2026-08-05 the alignment step is restricted to the chain direction
%     (parent claim target -> child credentialSubject root), so "same
%     credential" means one credential or a parent/child chain, never two
%     sibling credentials of one subject. Separating the claims into sibling
%     credentials is therefore a repair the predicate recognises.

import vc_metamodel.

% Revocation-pattern markers on claims (minimal added structure).
class StaticRevocation {
    Claim[1] target
}

class DynamicRevocation {
    Claim[1] target
}

% Co-location step: one hop through the credential/claim structure. Either a
% credential_statement edge (claim source<->target, via cred_neighbors) or a
% CHAIN-LINK alignment step: a parent credential's value node and a child
% credential's subject node trace the same domain Entity. Inlined rather than
% reusing the `aligned` shadow pred, which is display-only AND symmetric.
%
% Why cred_neighbors+ alone (the first draft) cannot work: cred_neighbors only
% connects the source and target of ONE claim, and Claim `contains` both ends,
% so a second claim can never attach to the same component without giving some
% CredEntity two containers (structurally inconsistent). Verified 2026-07-28
% and again 2026-08-05: the chained instance is `consistent` under the
% cred_neighbors-only variant, so the alignment step is load-bearing.
%
% Re-scoping 2026-08-05 (stress-test CLM-cross_layer_constraints_as_predicates finding F1, solver-confirmed): the
% alignment disjunct was previously unrestricted — any two distinct CredEntities
% tracing the same Entity. That made SIBLING credentials of one subject
% co-located (all CSOK credential subjects trace `Applicant`), so
% revocation_mismatch fired on the separated-credential design the paper
% prescribes as the remedy. The two added conjuncts make the chain direction
% structural: `s` must be a claim target (a value node INSIDE a credential) and
% `t` must be the credentialSubject root OF a credential. Two credential roots
% therefore never step to each other; a parent value stepping to a child root
% still does. Both conjuncts are positive atoms asserted verbatim in the
% instances, not negations over unclosed relations.
% Evidence: probe_c11_separated_design{,_control}.problem; see vault ART-c11_rescoping_probe_battery.
pred colocated_step(CredEntity s, CredEntity t) <->
    cred_neighbors(s, t)
  ; s != t,
    Claim::target(_, s),
    credentialSubject(_, t),
    CredEntity::trace(s, e),
    CredEntity::trace(t, e).

% Claim membership: cl belongs to the credential chain rooted at
% CredentialSubject r if its source is r itself or reachable from r through
% credential_statement edges and alignment steps (chained credentials).
pred claim_in_credential(Claim cl, CredentialSubject r) <->
    Claim::source(cl, r)
  ; Claim::source(cl, x), colocated_step+(r, x).

% First-draft variant, kept for the record (cannot fire; see comment above):
% pred claim_in_credential(Claim cl, CredentialSubject r) <->
%     Claim::source(cl, r)
%   ; Claim::source(cl, x), cred_neighbors+(r, x).

% C11: a credential (chain) must not bundle claims of mismatched revocation
% cadence. Two distinct claims reachable from one credential root, one marked
% static and one marked dynamic, are co-located -> error.
% Exercised 2026-07-28, re-exercised after the 2026-08-05 re-scoping: fires on
% revocation_mismatch_instance.problem, silent on
% revocation_mismatch_control.problem and on both separated-design probes
% (see header). "Credential (chain)" is now structural: co-location is
% bundling within one credential or across a parent/child chain link, never
% across sibling credentials of one subject.
error revocation_mismatch(Claim cl1, Claim cl2) <->
    cl1 != cl2,
    claim_in_credential(cl1, r),
    claim_in_credential(cl2, r),
    StaticRevocation::target(_, cl1),
    DynamicRevocation::target(_, cl2).

% --- Firing scenario (delivered 2026-07-28) ---
% revocation_mismatch_instance.problem: chained two-claim credential pair per
% the ED family (cred_0_1 describes the value of its parent cred_0_0), one
% claim StaticRevocation-marked, one DynamicRevocation-marked. check -k reports
% revocation_mismatch(claim_0_0, claim_0_1) as an inconsistency (UNSAT).
% revocation_mismatch_control.problem: identical structure, both claims
% static-marked. check -k reports "Model is consistent" (SAT).
